//// A variant's identity across a package boundary (front 00 · 04-js, D3).
////
//// The value is built HERE, in the consumer; the `case` that reads it lives
//// in the dependency. Nothing else in `tests/language/` puts those two on
//// opposite sides of a package boundary, and one package cannot see the
//// defect at all: inside a single package there is one copy of the enum's
//// classes and every lowering agrees.
////
//// What it pins: a payload-less variant is matched by the `tag` its
//// prototype carries, not by the JS class its singleton is an instance of.
//// commonJS used to test `_s instanceof __Token__Text__Size$X3xl` whenever
//// the variant's bare name was unique in the module, and `_s.tag === "Lg"`
//// when it repeated. A section's inner enum is re-emitted in every module
//// that names it, so the consumer's `.Text.Size.X3xl` was an instance of
//// the consumer's class and never of the library's: the arm did not fire,
//// no later arm did either, and the `case` answered `undefined` — printed,
//// at exit 0, with no diagnostic. `.Text.Size.Lg` printed correctly the
//// whole time, which is what kept it hidden.
////
//// The two lines below therefore have to answer the same KIND of thing:
//// a unique name and a repeated one, through the same dispatcher.

import {Token, styleOf} from "tokenlib";

pub fn main() {
    val unique: Token = .Text.Size.X3xl;
    val repeated: Token = .Text.Size.Lg;
    @print(styleOf(unique));
    @print(styleOf(repeated));
    @print(styleOf(.Text.Bold));
    @print(styleOf(.Bg.Lg));
}
